# The Top 100 Most Popular Bible Verses: A Composite Popularity Index (World English Bible)

## TL;DR

- **John 3:16 is the undisputed #1** — it tops Google search (~2,100,000 searches/month per World Vision UK’s 2022 Ahrefs study), TopVerses.com’s 37-million-reference internet index, YouVersion and BibleStudyTools historically, and was the single most-preached sermon verse of 2021 (Lifeway/Faithlife); no other verse is remotely close, out-searching #2 by roughly 25×.
- The **true top tier blends four signals**: raw search volume, platform engagement (Bible Gateway, YouVersion), memorization programs (Navigators, Awana, Fighter Verses), and cultural ubiquity (tattoos, sermons, merchandise) — which is why **Jeremiah 29:11, Philippians 4:13, Psalm 23, Romans 8:28, Proverbs 3:5-6, and Isaiah 41:10** all rank in the top 10.
- Major sources **disagree sharply below #1**: Bible Gateway’s 2024–2025 lists are dominated by the Psalm 23 and Psalm 91 clusters (Psalm 23:4 was #1 both years, and Psalm 23 + Psalm 91 claimed all but one of the top 23 spots), YouVersion crowns Isaiah 41:10 (its most-engaged verse four of the last six years), and search-volume data crowns Jeremiah 29:11 / Philippians 4:13 — this index reconciles those competing signals into a single blended order.

## Methodology & How Conflicts Were Reconciled

This ranking is a **composite index** weighting four data streams:

1. **Search volume (~35%)** — World Vision UK’s 2022 study (via Independent Catholic News), using the Ahrefs Keyword Explorer across 172 countries: **John 3:16 – 2,100,000 monthly searches; Jeremiah 29:11 and Philippians 4:13 – 82,000 each; John 10:10 – 73,000; Proverbs 3:5 – 58,000; Matthew 28:19 – 50,000; Philippians 4:8 – 42,000; Philippians 4:6 – 37,000; Romans 8:28 and James 1:3 – 33,000.** In the UK specifically, the top five were John 3:16 (6,500/mo), Jeremiah 29:11 (2,000), John 14:6 (1,900), Joshua 1:9 (1,800) and Genesis 1:1 (1,600).
1. **Platform engagement (~30%)** — Bible Gateway’s annual Top 100 (Psalm 23:4 was #1 in both 2024 and 2025); YouVersion’s Verse of the Year (Isaiah 41:10 named most-engaged verse four of the last six years, including 2023 and 2025; Philippians 4:6 in 2024); BibleStudyTools; and TopVerses.com, which ranked every verse against ~37 million online references (John 3:16 #1, John 1:1 #2, John 14:6 #3, Matthew 28:19 #4, Romans 3:23 #5).
1. **Memorization (~20%)** — the Navigators Topical Memory System (60 core verses), Awana, Truth78’s Fighter Verses, and “first verses to memorize” consensus lists from Desiring God, The Gospel Coalition, and Scott Slayton’s widely cited “First 15 Verses Every Christian Should Memorize.”
1. **Cultural penetration (~15%)** — most-tattooed verses (Philippians 4:13, Joshua 1:9, and Jeremiah 29:11 lead the tattoo lists), sermon databases (Lifeway/Faithlife found John 3:16 the most-preached verse of 2021, with 94% of all Bible verses appearing in at least one sermon that year), wedding/funeral readings (1 Corinthians 13; Psalm 23), and social-media sharing.

**Conflict resolution.** Where sources diverge, I favored consistency across independent streams over any single dataset. Bible Gateway’s list is heavily skewed by its own methodology — it counts short lookups, so whole chapters like Psalm 23 and Psalm 91 dominate (Isaiah 41:10, YouVersion’s #1, ranks only ~26th on Bible Gateway). I therefore treated Psalm 23 as a **distributed cluster** rather than allowing six Psalm 23 verses to occupy the top six slots. YouVersion’s Isaiah 41:10 (a highlight/bookmark/share engagement metric) was balanced against its comparatively modest search volume. TopVerses.com (which elevates John 1:1 and John 14:6 on historic citation weight) was blended down where modern search and engagement are weaker.

**Caveats up front:** all datasets are English-language-weighted and Protestant-leaning; search volume favors verses people have *not* already memorized; WEB’s “only born Son” wording differs from the NIV/KJV most people recognize; and cross-source numbers are not directly comparable (different years, metrics, and denominators).

## Cluster Notes

- **Psalm 23** (esp. 23:1, 23:4, 23:6): Bible Gateway’s top six verses in both 2024 and 2025 were all from Psalm 23 (order: 23:4, 23:1, 23:6, 23:5, 23:3, 23:2). Popularity is distributed across the whole psalm; I rank 23:4 and 23:1 highest and fold the rest into the cluster.
- **Psalm 91** (esp. 91:1, 91:11): surged on Bible Gateway in 2024–2025, and together with Psalm 23 claimed all but one of that platform’s top 23 spots — the reason John 3:16 fell to #24 *on Bible Gateway specifically* (not in the wider data).
- **Romans Road** (Romans 3:23, 6:23, 5:8, 10:9): the evangelism-memorization cluster, all ranking high on TopVerses.
- **John 3:16-17** and **Philippians 4:6-8** (4:6, 4:7, 4:8, 4:13): whole-passage popularity distributed across adjacent verses.
- **1 Corinthians 13:4-7**: the “love chapter,” dominant at weddings and #1 on DailyVerses.net.

